# Line endings are LF everywhere, matching .editorconfig's `end_of_line = lf`.
#
# Without this, a clone made with Git's Windows default (`core.autocrlf=true`)
# checks every text file out as CRLF. Nothing in the repository is stored that
# way — every committed blob is already LF — so the difference is invisible
# until something compares a file it read against text it generated.
#
# `scripts/release/sync-version.mjs --check` does exactly that: it reads the
# README and compares it to what Prettier would emit, which is LF. On a Windows
# clone the two never match and the check reports a README/phases.json drift
# that does not exist. CI only ever ran ubuntu-latest, so it was never seen
# until phase 20 added windows-latest to the matrix.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be line-ending converted. `text=auto` detects most of
# these on content, but naming them is cheaper than trusting the heuristic with
# an icon that a corrupted byte would silently break.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.msi binary
*.AppImage binary
*.deb binary
